"A 45-year-old woman is in the hospital 3 days after a total hip replacement. She has not walked since surgery because of pain. This morning she suddenly develops shortness of breath and sharp chest pain on the right that worsens with breathing. She takes an oral estrogen for menopausal symptoms. Temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 118/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. The lungs are clear. Her left calf is swollen. A chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Which of the following sets of arterial blood gas findings is most likely in this patient?",
# 46 sarcoid hypercalcemia
"A 35-year-old African American woman comes to the physician because of a dry cough, fatigue and mild shortness of breath for 3 months. She has also had painful red nodules on her shins. She works as a teacher and has never smoked. Temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F). There are tender, red nodules on both shins. Lung examination shows no abnormalities. A chest x-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show a serum calcium of 11.8 mg/dL, a low parathyroid hormone concentration, a high 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D concentration, and an increased serum angiotensin-converting enzyme activity. A transbronchial biopsy shows tight clusters of epithelioid histiocytes and multinucleated giant cells without central necrosis. Acid-fast and fungal stains are negative. Which of the following best explains this patient's hypercalcemia?",
# 47 INH B6
"A 42-year-old man who emigrated from the Philippines 2 years ago comes to the physician because of a productive cough, fever, night sweats and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss over 2 months. He drinks 4 beers daily. Temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F). There are crackles over the right upper lung. A chest x-ray shows a cavity in the right upper lobe. Sputum shows acid-fast bacilli, and an HIV test is negative. He is started on a four-drug regimen that includes isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol. Which of the following should also be given to prevent a common adverse effect of this treatment, peripheral neuropathy?",
# 48 small cell
"A 62-year-old man comes to the physician because of a cough for 2 months and confusion and headache for 1 week. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 50 years. He has lost 7 kg (15 lb) without trying. Pulse is 82/min and blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. He is euvolemic, without edema or orthostatic changes. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L, serum osmolality of 258 mOsm/kg, and urine osmolality of 560 mOsm/kg. Thyroid and adrenal function are normal. A CT scan of the chest shows a large mass near the right hilum with bulky mediastinal lymph nodes. A biopsy shows small, round blue cells with scant cytoplasm and crush artifact that stain positive for chromogranin. Which of the following is the most likely type of tumor?",
# 49 RDS
"A male newborn is delivered by cesarean section at 30 weeks' gestation because of preterm labor. His mother has type 1 diabetes with poorly controlled glucose during the pregnancy. She did not receive corticosteroids before delivery. Apgar scores are 6 and 7 at 1 and 5 minutes. Within 1 hour of birth he has grunting, nasal flaring and intercostal retractions. Respirations are 72/min, and oxygen saturation is 84% on room air. A chest x-ray shows diffuse fine granular opacities with air bronchograms and low lung volumes. Deficiency of a substance produced by which of the following cells is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
# 50 minimal change
"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his mother noticed that his eyelids have been puffy for 1 week and his legs became swollen 3 days ago. Two weeks ago he had an upper respiratory tract infection. Blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. There is swelling around both eyes and pitting edema of both legs and the scrotum. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood or casts. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL and serum cholesterol is 340 mg/dL. Serum creatinine and complement levels are normal. Light microscopy of a kidney biopsy shows normal glomeruli, and immunofluorescence shows no immune deposits. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on electron microscopy of the biopsy specimen?",
# 51 PSGN
"An 8-year-old girl is brought to the physician because her urine has been dark brown, like cola, for 2 days. Her mother noticed that her face looks puffy in the morning. Two weeks ago she had a sore throat with fever that resolved on its own. Blood pressure is 142/94 mm Hg. There is mild swelling of both eyelids and ankles. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein, many red blood cells and red blood cell casts. Serum creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL. Serum C3 concentration is decreased, and antistreptolysin O titer is increased. A kidney biopsy shows hypercellular glomeruli with many neutrophils; immunofluorescence shows granular deposits of IgG and C3. Which of the following is most likely to be seen on electron microscopy?",
# 52 furosemide
"A 72-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of worsening shortness of breath for 3 days. He has heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction from a previous myocardial infarction. He ate salty food at a family party last weekend. Pulse is 104/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg. Oxygen saturation is 88% on room air. Jugular veins are distended to the angle of the jaw. There are crackles over both lungs halfway up, and pitting edema of both legs to the knees. He is given intravenous furosemide, and he passes 2 L of urine over the next 3 hours. Which of the following is the site of action of this drug?",
# 53 NAGMA diarrhea
"A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of watery diarrhea, about 10 times daily, for 3 days after returning from a trip abroad. He has had mild abdominal cramps but no vomiting or blood in the stool. He takes no medications. Pulse is 108/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. His mucous membranes are dry. Laboratory studies show:\n\nSodium 140 mEq/L\nPotassium 3.1 mEq/L\nChloride 115 mEq/L\nBicarbonate 15 mEq/L\nGlucose 92 mg/dL\n\nArterial blood gas analysis on room air shows a pH of 7.30 and PaCO2 of 30 mm Hg. Which of the following best describes this patient's acid-base disorder?",
# 54 PAH clearance
"A research team is studying kidney blood flow in healthy volunteers. Each volunteer receives a continuous intravenous infusion of a test substance until the plasma concentration is stable. Timed urine samples and plasma samples are then collected. The substance is chosen because it is freely filtered at the glomerulus and almost all of the remaining amount in the peritubular capillaries is secreted by the proximal tubule, so that nearly none remains in renal venous blood. The clearance of this substance is calculated as urine concentration multiplied by urine flow, divided by plasma concentration. Which of the following substances is the team most likely using to estimate effective renal plasma flow?",
# 55 ADPKD
"A 40-year-old man comes to the physician because of dull bilateral flank pain and an episode of red urine for 2 months. He has had high blood pressure for 5 years that has been difficult to control. His mother died of a sudden, severe headache at age 48 years, and his maternal uncle receives dialysis. Blood pressure is 158/98 mm Hg. Large, irregular masses are felt in both flanks. A soft midsystolic click is heard at the apex. Urinalysis shows red blood cells. Serum creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL. Ultrasonography shows enlarged kidneys replaced by many cysts of various sizes, and several cysts in the liver. A mutation in which of the following genes is most likely responsible for this patient's condition?",
# 56 RCC
"A 60-year-old man comes to the physician because of blood in his urine and a dull ache in his left flank for 1 month. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years and is obese. Temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F) and blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg. A mass is palpable in the left flank. His face is ruddy. A left-sided scrotal varicocele is present that does not decompress when he lies down. Laboratory studies show a hematocrit of 56% and an increased serum erythropoietin concentration. A CT scan shows a 7-cm mass in the left kidney with tumor extending into the left renal vein. Which of the following best explains this patient's mass and his high hematocrit?",
# 57 celiac
"A 25-year-old woman comes to the physician because of bloating, frequent loose and foul-smelling stools, and fatigue for 1 year. She has also had an extremely itchy rash on her elbows and knees. She has type 1 diabetes. Her BMI is 18 kg/m2. There are clusters of small blisters and excoriations on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 10.1 g/dL and mean corpuscular volume of 74 μm3 with a low ferritin. Upper endoscopy with duodenal biopsy shows flattened villi, deep crypts and many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which of the following laboratory tests would most strongly support the most likely diagnosis?",
# 58 Crohn
"A 24-year-old man comes to the physician because of crampy pain in the right lower abdomen, watery diarrhea 5 times daily, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss over 4 months. He also has had drainage of pus near his anus. He smokes half a pack of cigarettes daily. Temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F). There is tenderness and fullness in the right lower quadrant. There is an opening beside the anus that drains pus. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL. Colonoscopy shows patchy, deep linear ulcers in the terminal ileum and right colon, separated by normal-looking mucosa, with a cobblestone appearance. The rectum appears normal. Biopsies show inflammation through all layers of the bowel wall and noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
# 59 HBV vaccinated
"A 26-year-old nursing student comes to the occupational health clinic for screening before beginning her clinical rotations. She feels well and has no history of jaundice or liver disease. She does not use drugs and has had one sexual partner. She received a series of injections as a child but does not know which ones. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. Serum alanine aminotransferase is normal. Hepatitis B studies show:\n\nHBsAg negative\nAnti-HBs positive\nAnti-HBc (total) negative\n\nWhich of the following best explains these results?",
# 60 H pylori urease
"A 45-year-old man comes to the physician because of burning pain in his upper abdomen for 2 months. The pain wakes him at night and improves when he eats. He takes no medications and does not use NSAIDs. He drinks alcohol occasionally. Vital signs are normal. There is mild epigastric tenderness. Upper endoscopy shows a 1-cm clean-based ulcer in the first part of the duodenum. Biopsies of the gastric antrum show chronic inflammation and curved, rod-shaped organisms on the epithelial surface. A breath test after he swallows urea labeled with carbon-13 shows increased labeled carbon dioxide. Which of the following bacterial enzymes allows this organism to survive in the stomach?",
# 61 PPI
"A 48-year-old man comes to the physician because of burning chest pain after meals and when lying down at night for 6 months. He often wakes with a sour taste in his mouth. Antacids help briefly. He is overweight and drinks 3 cups of coffee daily. Vital signs are normal. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. An ECG is normal. Upper endoscopy shows erosions of the distal esophagus without metaplasia. He is started on omeprazole taken 30 minutes before breakfast. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?",
# 62 Gilbert
"A 22-year-old woman comes to the student health clinic because her roommate noticed her eyes looked yellow this week. She is studying for final exams and has been skipping meals and sleeping little. She feels well otherwise and has no abdominal pain or dark urine. She remembers a similar episode when she had the flu last year. She does not drink alcohol and takes no medications. There is mild scleral icterus. The abdomen is nontender, and there is no hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory studies show a total bilirubin of 3.1 mg/dL, with a direct bilirubin of 0.3 mg/dL. Hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, lactate dehydrogenase, liver enzymes and alkaline phosphatase are within the reference ranges. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of these findings?",
# 63 Hirschsprung
"A male newborn is evaluated in the nursery because he has not passed meconium 48 hours after birth. He was born at term and is breastfeeding poorly; this morning he vomited green fluid. He has upslanting eyes, a flat facial profile and a single palmar crease. His abdomen is distended. The anal canal is in the normal position, and on digital rectal examination the rectum feels tight; as the finger is removed, there is an explosive release of stool and gas. A contrast enema shows a narrow rectum with a dilated colon above it. A rectal suction biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa. Which of the following is the most likely embryologic cause of this patient's condition?",
# 64 APAP
"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after she swallowed about 40 tablets of an over-the-counter pain reliever after an argument with her boyfriend. She has had nausea and vomited once but otherwise feels well. She takes no other medications. Pulse is 92/min and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. She is alert and anxious. The abdomen is soft with mild tenderness in the right upper quadrant. Serum aminotransferase levels are normal now, but her serum drug concentration plotted on a nomogram is well above the treatment line. Which of the following antidotes should be given now, and how does it work?",
# 65 Graves
"A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician because of nervousness, heat intolerance, palpitations and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss over 3 months despite a good appetite. She has been having 3 bowel movements daily. Her menstrual periods have become lighter. Pulse is 112/min and blood pressure is 142/62 mm Hg. Her eyes look prominent, with the upper lids pulled back, and there is swelling of the soft tissue around the eyes. The thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged and nontender, and a bruit is heard over it. There is a fine tremor of the outstretched hands. There are firm, raised plaques on the front of both shins. Serum TSH is undetectable and free T4 is high. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
# 66 Hashimoto
"A 50-year-old woman comes to the physician because of fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight gain over 6 months. Her hair has become coarse and her menstrual periods are heavier than before. Her sister has vitiligo. Pulse is 56/min. Her skin is dry and cool. The thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged, firm, rubbery and nontender. Ankle reflexes relax slowly. Serum TSH is 24 μU/mL and free T4 is low. Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase are present at a high titer. A fine-needle aspiration of the thyroid is performed. Which of the following findings is most likely on microscopy?",
# 67 DKA HSL
"A 14-year-old girl with type 1 diabetes is brought to the emergency department because of abdominal pain, vomiting and confusion for 1 day. She stopped taking insulin 3 days ago because she was worried about gaining weight. Pulse is 124/min, respirations are 32/min and deep, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. Her breath has a fruity odor and her mucous membranes are dry. Laboratory studies show a glucose of 520 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 9 mEq/L, an anion gap of 26 mEq/L, and a high serum beta-hydroxybutyrate concentration. Increased activity of which of the following enzymes in adipose tissue supplies the substrate for this patient's ketone production?",
# 68 metformin
"A 54-year-old man comes for a follow-up visit after a routine screening showed a fasting glucose of 152 mg/dL; a repeat test today shows a hemoglobin A1c of 7.6%. He works as a truck driver, eats mostly fast food and does not exercise. His BMI is 34 kg/m2. Blood pressure is 136/86 mm Hg. There are velvety, darkened patches on the back of his neck. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL. His physician recommends lifestyle changes and starts the first-line oral drug for his condition, explaining that it rarely causes low blood sugar but should be avoided if his kidney function drops sharply. Which of the following is the main mechanism of action of this drug?",
# 69 Cushing disease
"A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of weight gain, easy bruising and irregular periods for 1 year. She takes no medications. Blood pressure is 156/96 mm Hg. She has a round, red face, a fat pad on the upper back, and wide purple stretch marks on her abdomen. Her arms and legs are thin, and she has trouble rising from a chair without using her arms. Fasting glucose is 142 mg/dL. Her 24-hour urine cortisol is high, and cortisol is not suppressed after a low-dose overnight dexamethasone test. Her plasma ACTH concentration is high. After a high-dose dexamethasone test, her cortisol falls by more than 50%. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
# 70 21-hydroxylase
"A newborn is examined in the nursery. She was born at term to a 26-year-old woman after an uncomplicated pregnancy. Examination shows an enlarged clitoris and fused labial folds; a uterus is seen on ultrasonography. Her karyotype is 46,XX. On day 10 of life, she is brought back because of poor feeding, vomiting and lethargy. Pulse is 180/min and blood pressure is 50/28 mm Hg. Her skin is darker than expected. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L, potassium of 6.9 mEq/L and glucose of 48 mg/dL. Which of the following hormone precursors is most likely to be increased in this patient's serum?",
# 71 Conn
"A 42-year-old man comes to the physician because of muscle weakness and frequent nighttime urination. He has had high blood pressure for 2 years that has not come down despite taking amlodipine, lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide at full doses. He does not drink alcohol or eat black licorice. Blood pressure is 170/100 mm Hg. There is no edema. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 144 mEq/L, potassium of 2.9 mEq/L and bicarbonate of 32 mEq/L. Plasma aldosterone concentration is high and plasma renin activity is low. A CT scan of the abdomen shows a 1.5-cm mass in the left adrenal gland. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
# 72 pheochromocytoma
"A 35-year-old woman comes to the physician because of episodes of severe pounding headache, heavy sweating and palpitations, each lasting 10 to 20 minutes, several times a week for 3 months. During an episode in the clinic, her blood pressure is 210/120 mm Hg and pulse is 128/min; between episodes her blood pressure is 148/94 mm Hg. She is pale and has a fine tremor during the episode. Her mother had a thyroid cancer removed at age 40 years. Plasma free metanephrine concentrations are markedly increased. A CT scan shows a 4-cm mass in the right adrenal gland. Surgery is planned. Which of the following drugs should be started first to prepare her for surgery?",
# 73 PCOS
"A 26-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has had only 4 menstrual periods in the past year. She also has acne and dark, coarse hair growing on her chin and abdomen. She and her husband have been trying to conceive for 1 year. Her BMI is 33 kg/m2. Blood pressure is 132/84 mm Hg. There are velvety, dark patches in her armpits. Pelvic examination shows no masses. A pregnancy test is negative. Serum LH concentration is increased, with an LH-to-FSH ratio of 3:1. Serum testosterone is mildly increased and prolactin and TSH are normal. Ultrasonography shows enlarged ovaries with more than 20 small follicles around the edge of each. If her condition is not treated, she is at greatest risk of which of the following cancers?",
# 74 Klinefelter
"A 20-year-old man comes to the physician because he and his wife have been unable to conceive for 1 year. He reports that he never had much facial hair and he was teased at school for having \"breasts.\" He had learning difficulties in reading. He is 191 cm (6 ft 3 in) tall, with long arms and legs. There is bilateral breast enlargement with glandular tissue under the nipples. Pubic hair is sparse. Both testes are small (2 mL) and firm. Semen analysis shows no sperm. Serum FSH and LH concentrations are high and testosterone concentration is low. Which of the following is the most likely karyotype of this patient?",
# 75 hCG
"A 29-year-old woman comes to the physician 6 weeks after her last menstrual period because of nausea in the mornings and breast tenderness. A home pregnancy test was positive. Transvaginal ultrasonography shows a single intrauterine gestational sac with a yolk sac and an embryo with cardiac activity. A cystic structure is seen in the right ovary. The physician explains that in early pregnancy, progesterone needed to maintain the uterine lining is still made mainly by the ovary, until the placenta takes over. Which of the following hormones maintains this ovarian progesterone production, and which cells produce it?",
# 76 trastuzumab
"A 48-year-old woman comes to the physician after a biopsy of a 3-cm mass in her left breast showed invasive ductal carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry shows that the tumor is negative for estrogen and progesterone receptors and strongly positive (3+) for HER2. One axillary lymph node contains tumor. She has no history of heart disease, and her current left ventricular ejection fraction is 62%. Her treatment plan includes chemotherapy and a monoclonal antibody directed against the HER2 receptor, given for 1 year. Which of the following tests should be monitored regularly during treatment because of a known adverse effect of this antibody?",
# 77 Turner
"A 15-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has not started having menstrual periods. She is shorter than all of her classmates. As a newborn she had swelling of the hands and feet. She is 140 cm (4 ft 7 in) tall (below the 3rd percentile). She has a low posterior hairline, a webbed neck, a broad chest with widely spaced nipples, and no breast development. Her blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the right leg. Serum FSH and LH concentrations are high. Pelvic ultrasonography shows a small uterus and streak ovaries. Her karyotype is 45,X. Which of the following heart defects is most common in patients with this condition?",
# 78 prolactinoma
"A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period for 6 months and has noticed milky discharge from both breasts. She has also had headaches and decreased interest in sex. She has not been pregnant and takes no medications. Vital signs are normal. Milky fluid can be expressed from both nipples. Visual fields are full. A urine pregnancy test is negative. Serum prolactin concentration is 210 ng/mL (N < 25). TSH and free T4 are normal. An MRI of the brain shows an 8-mm mass in the anterior pituitary. Which of the following drugs is the most appropriate first-line treatment?",
# 79 scurvy
"A 74-year-old widower is brought to the physician by his son because of fatigue and bleeding from his gums when he brushes his teeth for 2 months. Since his wife died a year ago, he has eaten mostly tea, toast, crackers and canned soup. He drinks 3 beers daily. He has had several bruises on his legs without injury, and a cut on his hand has not healed after 4 weeks. His gums are swollen, purple and bleed easily. There are small red-purple spots around hair follicles on his thighs, with coiled, corkscrew-shaped hairs. Platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Which of the following biochemical reactions is most likely impaired in this patient?",
# 80 kwashiorkor
"A 2-year-old girl is brought to a clinic in a famine-affected region because of poor appetite and swelling of her legs for 3 weeks. After her younger brother was born, she was weaned from breastfeeding and has been fed mostly a thin porridge made from cassava. She is irritable and apathetic. Her weight is at the 15th percentile. Her abdomen is protuberant, and the liver edge is palpable 4 cm below the costal margin. There is pitting edema of both legs and feet. Her hair is thin, sparse and reddish, and there are areas of peeling, darkened skin on her legs. Serum albumin is 1.9 g/dL. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
# 81 LPS
"A 68-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of fever, chills and confusion for 1 day. She has had burning urination for 4 days. Temperature is 39.7°C (103.5°F), pulse is 122/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 78/42 mm Hg despite 2 L of intravenous fluid. Her skin is warm and flushed. There is tenderness at the right costovertebral angle. Urinalysis shows many leukocytes and nitrites. Blood and urine cultures grow lactose-fermenting, gram-negative rods. Serum lactate is 5.2 mmol/L, and serum TNF-alpha and IL-6 concentrations are markedly increased. Which of the following bacterial components triggers this patient's systemic response, and through which receptor?",
# 82 follicular lymphoma
"A 58-year-old woman comes to the physician because of painless lumps in her neck and groin that have slowly grown and shrunk over 2 years. She feels well and has no fever, night sweats or weight loss. Examination shows nontender, rubbery lymph nodes, 1 to 3 cm in size, in the cervical, axillary and inguinal regions. The spleen tip is palpable. Complete blood count is normal. An excisional biopsy of an inguinal node shows crowded, uniform follicles throughout the node that lack tingible-body macrophages. The follicles contain small cells with cleaved nuclei that express CD10 and CD20. Cytogenetic analysis shows t(14;18). Which of the following best describes the effect of this translocation?",
# 83 X-linked dominant
"A genetic counselor evaluates a family with a rare disorder that causes short stature, bowing of the legs and low serum phosphate despite adequate vitamin D intake. The pedigree shows that an affected father has had 3 daughters and 3 sons with an unaffected woman. All 3 daughters are affected, and none of the sons are affected. One affected daughter has had 4 children with an unaffected man: 1 affected son, 1 unaffected son, 1 affected daughter and 1 unaffected daughter. Which of the following is the most likely mode of inheritance of this disorder?",
# 84 Hardy-Weinberg
"A couple who are both from an isolated island population come for genetic counseling before planning a pregnancy. Neither has any symptoms or a family history of disease. On their island, 1 in 10,000 children is born with an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder that causes intellectual disability if not detected early. The population is large, mating is random, and there is no significant migration or new mutation, so it is assumed to be in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Which of the following is closest to the frequency of carriers of this disorder in the island population?",
# 85 Gaucher
"A 25-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish man comes to the physician because of fatigue, easy bruising and aching pain in his thighs for 1 year. Pulse is 84/min. There is pallor. The spleen is palpable 12 cm below the left costal margin, and the liver is palpable 5 cm below the right costal margin. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 10.2 g/dL and platelet count of 64,000/mm3. An x-ray of the femurs shows flaring of the distal ends in an Erlenmeyer flask shape and areas of thinning. A bone marrow biopsy shows large macrophages with fibrillar cytoplasm that resembles crumpled tissue paper. Deficiency of which of the following enzymes is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
# 86 Li-Fraumeni
"A 30-year-old woman comes to the physician for genetic counseling after being diagnosed with breast cancer. Her younger brother was treated for an osteosarcoma of the leg at age 16 years and later developed leukemia. Her mother died of a brain tumor at age 40 years, and her maternal grandfather had an adrenal cortical carcinoma in his 30s. Her paternal family history is unremarkable. She has no café-au-lait spots, colon polyps or skin findings. A germline mutation in which of the following genes is most likely in this family?",
# 87 AL amyloidosis
"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of fatigue, swelling of his legs and shortness of breath for 4 months. He has noticed that his tongue seems too large for his mouth, and he has had numbness in both hands. Blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg, and he becomes light-headed when he stands. His tongue is enlarged with tooth marks on its edges. There are purple bruises around his eyes. There is pitting edema to the knees. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein. Serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal spike, and urine contains free lambda light chains. Echocardiography shows thick ventricular walls with a sparkling appearance. A fat pad biopsy shows deposits that are apple-green under polarized light after Congo red staining. Which of the following proteins is most likely deposited?",
# 88 zero-order
"In a pharmacology laboratory, healthy volunteers each receive a single dose of one of five substances, and plasma concentrations are measured every hour. For four of the substances, the concentration falls by the same fraction each hour; for example, it halves every 4 hours whether the level is high or low. For one substance, the concentration falls by the same amount each hour, about 15 to 20 mg/dL, until it is almost gone, and when the dose is doubled it takes about twice as long to clear. The students note that this substance has no constant half-life. Which of the following substances most likely shows this elimination pattern?",
# 89 PPV
"A company develops a new blood test to screen for colorectal cancer. To evaluate it, investigators test 300 adults who also undergo colonoscopy. Colonoscopy, the gold standard, shows that 100 of the participants have colorectal cancer and 200 do not. The new blood test is positive in 90 of the participants with cancer and in 20 of the participants without cancer. Which of the following is the positive predictive value of the blood test in this study population?",
# 90 NNT
"A randomized controlled trial evaluates a new anticoagulant for preventing stroke in 4,000 patients with atrial fibrillation. Half of the patients receive the new drug and half receive placebo. All patients are followed for 5 years. A stroke occurs in 20% of the patients in the placebo group and 15% of the patients in the treatment group. The new drug costs $400 per month. Based on these results, how many patients need to be treated with the new drug for 5 years to prevent one stroke?",
# 91 case-control
"Investigators want to know whether drinking coffee is associated with pancreatic cancer. They identify 200 patients with newly diagnosed pancreatic cancer at 5 hospitals and 400 patients without cancer who were admitted to the same hospitals for other reasons, matched by age and sex. Trained interviewers ask all participants how many cups of coffee they drank per day over the past 10 years. The investigators then compare the proportion of heavy coffee drinkers in the two groups. Which of the following measures of association is most appropriate for this study?",
# 92 type I error
"A pharmaceutical company conducts a randomized controlled trial of a new drug for patients with heart failure. The study uses a significance level of 0.05. The investigators report that 30-day mortality was lower in the drug group than in the placebo group (p = 0.04), and they conclude that the drug reduces mortality. Several years later, three large, well-designed trials and a meta-analysis show that the drug has no effect on mortality. Assuming the later studies are correct, which of the following best describes the error in the original study?",
# 93 lead-time bias
"A health system introduces a new low-dose CT screening program for lung cancer in heavy smokers. Before the program, patients were diagnosed when they developed symptoms, at an average age of 68 years, and lived an average of 1 year after diagnosis. After the program, cancers are diagnosed at an average age of 66 years, and patients live an average of 3 years after diagnosis. However, the average age at death from lung cancer is 69 years in both periods, and the lung cancer death rate has not changed. Which of the following best explains the apparent improvement in survival?",
# 94 minor contraception
"A 16-year-old girl comes to the physician alone and asks for a prescription for oral contraceptive pills. She has had one male sexual partner for 6 months, and they use condoms most of the time. She has no medical problems, does not smoke, and her blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. She asks the physician not to tell her parents because they would be angry. She lives in a state where minors may consent to contraceptive care. Which of the following is the most appropriate response by the physician?",
# 95 Jehovah's Witness
"A 40-year-old man develops heavy bleeding 6 hours after surgery to remove part of his colon. His hemoglobin has fallen from 13 g/dL to 6.8 g/dL, pulse is 124/min and blood pressure is 88/52 mm Hg. He is alert and oriented. The surgeon explains that he needs a blood transfusion and that he may die without it. He is a Jehovah's Witness and says clearly that he understands the risk of death, but he will not accept blood or blood products because of his religious beliefs. He signed an advance directive stating this before surgery. His wife, who is not a Jehovah's Witness, begs the team to transfuse him. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?",
# 96 interpreter
"A 58-year-old woman who recently arrived from Afghanistan comes to the clinic to discuss the results of her breast biopsy, which shows cancer. She speaks only Dari. Her 12-year-old son, who speaks fluent English and Dari, comes with her and offers to translate. A nurse on the unit says she learned some Dari from her grandparents and could help. The clinic has access to telephone and video interpreter services. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to communicate with this patient?",
# 97 SPIKES
"A 54-year-old woman comes to the physician's office to receive the results of a liver biopsy that she had last week after a CT scan showed several masses in her liver. The biopsy shows metastatic adenocarcinoma, most likely from the pancreas. The physician has arranged a quiet private room, turned off her pager, and invited the patient's husband to join at the patient's request. The patient sits down and says, \"I've been so worried all week.\" Which of the following is the most appropriate next step by the physician?",
